00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.030 align:start position:0%
 
ghost of Tsushima is something entirely  new for Sucker Punch productions it  draws from real world Japanese history  and explores it through the lens of  classical samurais cinema if you watch  the state of play gameplay demo you'll  know there's plenty to sink your sword  into so we spoke to create a director  Nate Foxx to learn more about the game  here's a few interesting things about  ghosts of Tsushima that you may not  already know your gym ball Sucker Punch  hasn't been shy about talking about his  inspirations for ghosts of Tsushima as a  game inspired by classical samurai  fiction and cinema it'll come as no  surprise that the work of the claimed  film director Akira Kurosawa was a key  point of reference for the game Seven  Samurai in particular was a major touch  though however the idea for the game  originally came from a comic book series  called a saga Yojimbo the series which  began in 1984 and is still going strong  is set in the Edo period and features a  rabbit Ronin named Miyamoto Usagi a play  on the legendary Miyamoto Musashi the  series features anthropomorphic animals  instead of humans and creative director  named Fox read it to get a feel for how  to write Sly Cooper he said in an  interview with GameSpot so I'm reading  the saga Yojimbo and it's this big  collection of stories about a samurai  who wanders feudal Japan walks into town  and uses his wits and the edge of his  sword to solve problems I thought man  this will be a dynamite thing and now we  have ghosts of Tsushima Nate said he's  also seen the anime version of rurouni  kenshin so I've got my fingers crossed  for a he talking about toes I easter-egg  respect the blade as well as drawing  stylistic inspiration from Kurosawa's  work Sucker Punch also looked to other  conventions in samurai cinema and try to  work them into ghosts of Tsushima this  includes the swordplay which is all  about quick lethal strikes instead of  whittling away at enemy health bars the  team has also put a lot of effort into  the way nature is portrayed which is why  we got a healthy helping of beautiful  scenery and animals in the state of play  video the other thing you may have  noticed in the gameplay demo is that  after killing the Mongols main character  Jin briefly bowels this is because  sucker punch is also making sure to  represent the respect that is traded  between samurai other swordsmen and  peasants Nate said a lot of samurai  identity  he comes very much from samurai film  however we wanted to do right by  providing a feeling of authenticity and  that meant admitting that a bunch of  American developers did not know what  they were talking about and drawing in  experts from many different fields to  teach us what it was like at that time  we have experts on religion on costume  on motion we brought them in early and  often to review the game is those  partnerships that give you the feeling  of wandering through feudal Japan of  course gin shows respect when he's  operating as a samurai and abiding by  the samurai code in the underhanded  ghost way of life however respect may  not come so readily wind beneath my  wings  one of the core features that  sucker-punch showed off was how wisps of  wind would serve to guide players  towards markers and objectives  I asked Nate how they approach making  players feel like they're finding things  naturally without feeling the invisible  hand of someone pushing them his  response was that the wind is just an  indicator and in fact the game is  designed to distract you with other  things you can choose to pursue in the  state of play demo for example there was  a bear mauling someone and you can  choose to get involved in that you might  also come across a group of Mongols  raiding a farm and you can decide  whether you want to step in in that  scenario they actually said that he  hopes that place don't always follow the  guiding winds as there's a lot to  experience off the beaten path when you  choose to set the wind to go somewhere  that doesn't mean you have to do it in  fact I would hope that you don't I would  hope that you find yourself looking at  hill and seeing some fire and thinking  well somebody lit that fire I don't know  if they're a friend or a foe but  somebody's up there and when you go up  there you're rewarded with content or a  secret that makes your curiosity  justified basically he's saying you're a  wandering Samurai so make sure you  wander live by the sword one question I  had for Nate was whether Jin's  experiences inevitably end in some sort  of combat scenario and while he was  careful about not spoiling anything he  indicated that not every problem will be  solved by the blade sure there's a fair  amount of combat in the game it's a core  gameplay pillar after all but there's  variety beyond that he said there's more  to life than just sword fighting in  ghosts of Tsushima there's also a lot of  people in need and a lot of ways in  which you can serve them this game while  it tracks Jin's trajectory from the  samurai to giving up his identity to  become the go  there are also characters you'll meet  along the way that if you decide to you  can get invested in their stories and in  helping them out it's part of discovery  the overall mission gin is on is to  drive the Mongols out of Tsushima and  save the people but that isn't always  done through burning down shipyards like  in this day of play demo sometimes it  can be rescuing a kidnapped loved one  finding cherished items lost as people  flee danger or helping people survive by  getting them food they describe ghosts  of Tsushima as a wartime epic where you  get to know a lot of people who are  struggling to survive inside a very  harsh landscape here's hoping the  smaller struggles are as impactful as  the bigger ones samurai Champloo the  quick sword slices in the demo made me  think that the combat had a Batman style  counter mechanic so I asked Nate about  that he confirmed that the game  definitely features counters that  require you to have the patience and the  skill to read what the enemy is going to  do and unleash a really devastating  counter-attack however that's not the  only way you can play you can also force  the attack and go after an enemy it's  not just a waiting game but it is a very  skill based experience it wouldn't be a  video game in 2020 without some sort of  reference to a from software game so  Harry is the sole play is kind of like  Sakura in their enemies unleash lethal  attacks they said that this needed to be  the case to make it in line with samurai  cinema but also for realism he said to  feel like those natural samurai movie  Belle's enemies have to die with a few  strokes of a katana otherwise it's not a  katana it's a nerf tube that means that  when they hit you with swords you are  also going to die in a few strikes it's  very challenging Nate mentioned that of  course you're gonna have a bunch of  abilities to give you the edge and  you'll be able to spec out in both  samurai and ghost play styles and switch  between them also you've got a bow and  arrow  it might not be as deadly as some of the  explosives that the Mongols have but it  does a trick  true grit a couple of other things on  combi you may have seen gin raise his  sword at one point  yes gin has a variety of styles you'll  be able to use the combat the Mongols he  was raised and taught in a traditional  samurai style but when the Mongols  arrived and used weapons that are  completely unfamiliar to him he has to  adapt these stances are the new ways  that Jin has come up with to put himself  on equal footing with the Mongols their  stances you choose are part of the  tactical decision-making process  you may also have seen the UI elements  at the bottom of the gameplay video and  wonder what that stuff represents or the  red bar is held for naturally but the  golden spheres represent Jin's resolves  he can use them to regain a bit of  health and they're also a representation  of the grit he has and needs to pull off  the more devastating attacks there's a  risk versus reward element to these  attacks so you have to be thoughtful in  using them and then finally you may have  picked up on the fact that the original  paris games week reveal of Tsushima had  one-on-one duels but the state of play  didn't show any of that don't worry  they're still in the game and based on  what Nate said about them they sound  like intense focused yields between two  warriors that can be decided in the  blink of an eye  a big part of samurai films are duels he  said it's a classic it's two highly  trained warriors meeting and it's going  to be an apex challenge in the game  there are a number of them in goes of  Tsushima where you have to study the  person you're dueling how they attack  how they move read attacks counter and  then look for a window these are the  most challenging encounters you have in  the game and it's just one on one  so basically sharpen those reaction  times there you have it a few cool  ghosts of Tsushima effects that we  learned from a chat with the game's  creative director and Nate Fox if you  haven't already seen the gameplay demo  from state of play check it out on the  channel or on GameSpot calm otherwise  let me know what your favorite samurai  fiction is in the comments below or find  me on twitter at 2 more h  you

